why should business care and then  secondly can we kind of unpack a little  bit which businesses should care more or  less and why in terms of which  businesses which sectors have the  greatest impacts on biodiversity and I  put the plus or minus there because  those impacts are not always negative  business can have positive impacts on  biodiversity and ecosystems as well as  negative impacts and of course business  many of them also depend on biodiversity  and ecosystem services and I'll go into  that in a bit more detail broadly  speaking I think we can talk about  internal reasons internal to a business  and external pressures of various kinds  now internal business drivers I've  included consumer preference because  businesses are very focused on meeting  the demands of their customers but also  we need to think about the awareness or  the concern of the managers of a  business themselves because they're part  of this story and they're reading the  the press and other media just as the  rest of us are so they know increasingly  what's going on they're concerned about  it they're also very focused on what  their customers want which may or may  not have something to do with the  environment and they're also concerned  with what their investors want  particularly publicly listed companies  whose shares are sold in the market they  need to pay close attention to what  shareholders are saying in terms of  external pressures there's general  public concern about the environment  increasingly stringent product standards  as well as regulatory requirements so  I'm distinguishing here between  voluntary standards and legal  requirements and more and more  governments are looking to business for  answers to the biodiversity crisis  looking a little bit more closely at  what's going on within business  management and as part of the teeb study  about a year and two months ago we had  the great good fortune to have McKinsey  come to us and say could we do a survey  as a contribution to teeb and  use their contacts in the business world  about 1500 senior executives from around  the world to try and understand what our  business attitudes towards what our  managers attitudes towards biodiversity  and ecosystems and we had to spend a lot  of time trying to translate the the  language of biodiversity and ecosystems  into something that would be meaningful  to these executives hopefully we did  that so that part of the survey was  defining our terms and this is just one  example from the results suggesting that  the the leading reason that these  executives identified biodiversity as a  possible concern to their business was  reputation they want to ensure that they  are seen as good corporate citizens that  they are not seen as the bad guys now  the question is how important is that  really and what are they doing about it  that's another question but certainly  reputation was top of the list of the  reasons that the executives identified  for why they should care but the the  next two down are also interesting one  is the alignment with the company's  business goals mission or values and  there again I think we tend to forget  sometimes that people in business are us  often they're the same they're part of  society they want their companies to be  doing the right thing they want their  business to be contributing to their  their personal goals they want some  alignment or consistency between what  their business is doing and what they  they believe in and I think we shouldn't  discount that as a real driver of change  and thirdly not surprisingly responding  to regulatory requirements many  businesses see increasingly stringent  rules and regulations new laws coming on  the books related to biodiversity and  ecosystems in many different countries  and of course they have to respond to  that  you
 

